# .env.backend-secrets.example — secrets only the backend needs.
#
#   cp .env.backend-secrets.example .env.backend-secrets
#   # move these values OUT of .env and INTO the new file
#   docker compose up
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `docker-compose.yml` used to hand the whole of `.env` to three services:
# `proxy`, `dashboard-backend`, and `hermes`. The first two ARE the application
# and legitimately read most of it. `hermes` is a sidecar — it runs the same
# agent brain, so it genuinely needs the LLM provider keys, but it never issues
# a JWT, authenticates an admin, pushes to GitHub, or talks to Telegram.
#
# Handing it those anyway means a single compromise of the Hermes container —
# a container whose whole job is executing model-authored work — yields the
# GitHub PAT and the JWT signing secret. That is the credential over-exposure
# Docker AI Governance's credential surface exists to stop, and the reason
# sandbox profiles use an `env_allowlist` rather than a pass-through.
#
# Splitting the file is what applies the scoping. Until you do, `.env` still
# supplies everything and behaviour is unchanged — the compose entries are
# marked `required: false` precisely so this stays opt-in.
#
# NOTE: the runtime sidecars that execute model-authored code (opencode, goose,
# aider, jcode, task-harness, mcp-server) were already scoped to explicit
# `environment:` lists and never received `.env` at all. This file closes the
# remaining gap, it is not the whole story.
#
# Verify after splitting:
#   docker compose exec hermes printenv | grep -E 'GH_PAT|SECRET_KEY|TELEGRAM'
#   # → should print nothing
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Session signing and admin access ────────────────────────────────────────
# Forging a JWT with this is a full authentication bypass.
SECRET_KEY=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_EMAIL=

# ── GitHub write access ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A PAT with push rights is a supply-chain compromise if it leaks.
GH_PAT=

# ── Social login client secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Bot / service control ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SERVICE_TOKEN authorises mutating control over Telegram.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
SERVICE_TOKEN=

# ── Platform / infrastructure ───────────────────────────────────────────────
RENDER_API_KEY=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DELIBERATELY NOT HERE — these stay in `.env`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LLM provider keys (NVIDIA_API_KEY, CEREBRAS_API_KEY, GROQ_API_KEY,
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, …) and E2B_API_KEY: Hermes runs the agent brain and the
# sandbox path, so it needs them to do its job. Moving them here would break it.
#
# MONGO_URL: shared storage, read by more than the backend.
#
# The point of the split is least privilege, not maximum separation — moving a
# credential a service actually needs just produces a broken service and an
# operator who reverts the whole thing.
